@node Common description
@chapter Description of the PNGLoader library

@emph{PNGLoader} library is used to load PNG images from file into buffer with
PNG file header. It is lite and OpenGL friendly rather than libPNG.

Here the main features of the PNGLoader:

@itemize @minus
@item Small size of memory usage (about 35kB),
@item Simple functioning. Except some functions like saving image to the PNG
      file (rather than libPNG),
@item Possible to load PNG image from last row (useful for texture loading),
@item Simple programming interface (only one function to call)
@item Licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License v3 (LGPLv3).
@end itemize

Common restrictions:

@itemize @minus
@item Can not load palette PNG images,
@item Skip auxiliary chunks
@item Depend on side library (zlib)
@end itemize

@node Library usage
@chapter Usage of the PNGLoader library

Include @file{pngloader.h} into source code to use PNGLoader library functions.

@example
#include <pngloader.h>
@end example

Add @code{-lpngloader} to linker flags when linking with the library. When
using the shared library, link with the appropriate import library on Windows,
or pass the shared library name on Unix-like systems. If zlib symbols are
required, also add @code{-lz}.

@node Types and functions
@chapter Functions and types

@deftp {Data type} png_error_t
@code{png_error_t} describes PNGLoader error type. Possible values are in
@ref{table:png_error_t}.

@float table,table:png_error_t
@caption{Values of @code{png_error_t} type}
@multitable @columnfractions 0.5 0.5
@headitem Name @tab Description
@item @code{PNG_NO_ERROR} @tab This value always 0 and depicts success
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_NO_SUCH_FILE} @tab This error code depicts file access
      error. This value can return on file missing, file blocking or some other
      input-output troubles
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_NOT_PNG} @tab This error code has been returned when
      module decided that loaded file is not meet PNG specification
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_WRONG_CHUNK_ORDER} @tab Rare error, that returned when
      IDAT chunk has been met before IHDR chunk. This situation occurs when
      PNG file structure was misformed and size of image can not be defined
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_MALLOC} @tab This error occurs when dynamic memory can
      not be allocated. Usually this error occurs when PNG header has wrong
      image size or other chunk has bad length field
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_EMPTY_IMAGE} @tab This error indicates that IDAT chunk
      missing or empty (this is acceptable by PNG specification but does not
      make sense)
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED} @tab This error occurs when PNG file
      format is not supported by current version of PNGLoader. See
      @ref{Common description}
@item @code{PNG_ERROR_UNCOMPRESS} @tab This error returns when deflate stream
      can not be decompressed after load from PNG file. This error frequently
      occurs when PNG file is corrupted
@end multitable
@end float
@end deftp

@deftp {Data type} png_header_t
describes PNG file header. It's fields shown in @ref{table:png_header_t}.

@float table,table:png_header_t
@caption{@code{png_header_t} structure fields}
@multitable @columnfractions 0.25 0.25 0.5
@headitem Name @tab Type @tab Description
@item width @tab uint32_t @tab Width of the image (px)
@item height @tab uint32_t @tab Height of the image (px)
@item depth @tab uint8_t @tab Bit depth of the channel
@item color_type @tab uint8_t @tab Image type from PNG specification
@item compression @tab uint8_t @tab Compression type from PNG specification
@item filter @tab uint8_t @tab Filter type from PNG specification
@item interlace @tab uint8_t @tab Using of interlace from PNG specification
@end multitable
@end float
@end deftp

@deftypefun  png_error_t LoadPNGFromFile ( const char* @code{file_name}, png_header_t* @code{header}, uint8_t** @code{data}, bool @code{flip} )
load PNG from @var{file_name} file into memory pointed by @var{data}.
@var{header} variable holds content of IHDR chunk with PNG data. Image is
loaded from bottom to top if @var{flip} is set to @code{true}.
@code{LoadPNGFromFile} return error code with @code{png_error_t} type.

@code{data} variable is a pointer to the pointer. Memory is allocated
automatically on success. Use @code{FreePNG} to safely release memory.

The @code{header} must point to the @code{png_header_t} allocated variable.
It's content will be overwritten on function @code{LoadPNGFromFile} call. An
undefined content will be in @code{header} on error.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun png_error_t LoadPNGFromArray (const uint8_t* @code{raw_data}, size_t @code{length}, png_header_t* @code{header}, uint8_t** @code{data}, bool @code{flip})
This function is the same as @code{LoadPNGFromFile} except the source of the
PNG. The source for this function is plain array of bytes. @var{raw_data} is a
pointer to PNG data in memory. @var{length} represent size of the memory image
of the PNG.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void FreePNG (uint8_t** @code{data})
@code{FreePNG} function safely free memory after @code{LoadPNGFromFile} or
@code{LoadPNGFromArray} was completed. This function must be called with
@var{data} argument after successful complete of @code{LoadPNG} function if
data not needed anymore.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void PNGLoaderVersion (char* @code{str}, size_t @code{n})
@code{PNGLoaderVersion} function extract module version in @samp{N.N.N.N} form,
where N is a number of version component. Version string will be copied into
@var{str} with null symbol at the end. To prevent buffer overflow @var{n}
argument must contain length of the buffer. Version strings are typically short
(for example @samp{0.2.0.1}); callers should pass the buffer length in @var{n}
to avoid overflow.
@end deftypefun

